A1. What his employer gave him when he asked for a raise. ~ GATE
A5. A household fixture that has made Saturday night famous. ~ BATHTUB
A10. About the smallest thing there is—even smaller than some Congressmen. ~ ATOM
A15. The highest point. ~ APEX
A16. A division of the United Provinces (British India)—10,159 sq. miles. ~ AGRA
A17. What you do during an after-dinner speech. ~ YAWN
A20. Three letters like this one and Rastus leaves town. ~ K
A21. The time of the year when you kid yourself by swearing off the things you don't like to do anyway: ~ LENT
A22. No two sardines could get this way. ~ NEARER
A24. This one's pronounced the same as an insect that makes honey. ~ BE
A25. Another place besides a Klan meeting where you'll find sheets. ~ BEO
A27. This one often keeps company with "either." ~ OR
A28. A State close to the Mexican border. Not so old either (abr.). ~ NS
A29. Where your hat won't stay on a windy day. ~ ON
A31. A joint made by heating and hammering (not a restaurant). ~ WELD
A34. The second note that the would-be opera singer upstairs warbles just as the baby gets to sleep. ~ RA
A35. A dinky adverb meaning therefore. ~ SO
A36. Used to be an appetizer, but to-day they are guzzled all the time. ~ COCKTAIL
A37. This is the design worked in the lower left-hand and the upper right-hand corner of the puzzle. ~ HA
A39. A Southern State that makes a lot of noise about its peaches (abr.). ~ GA
A40. Don't be selfish, No. 29 Horizontal told you all about this one. ~ ON
A41. It's a good thing to know how to act in one—like when the wife comes in and finds the stenog. on your lap. ~ EMERGENCY
A46. A lubricant for wheatcakes. ~ SYRUP
A48. A possessive pronoun. ~ MY
A49. What you'd like to do about the "Follies." ~ GO
A51. Harangue. ~ TIRADE
A53. A fever attended by chills and shaking. ~ AGUE
A54. A highway (abr.). ~ ST
A55. What the cannibal did to the missionary. ~ ATE
A56. That chum of eithers again. ~ OR
A57. The first two of three letters often written on little slips of paper after a poker game has been on a few hours. ~ RO
A58. What you get besides pepper when you say "please pass the pepper." ~ SALT
A60. The kind of a crossing where motorists come to their own conclusions (abr.). ~ RR
A61. A metal—(have you had yours to-day?) ~ IRON
A63. When you can't cure a thing, you have to do this. ~ ENDURE
A67. Every X-word puzzle has to have this one somewhere—"to exist." ~ BE
A68. Most anyone knows enough Spanish to say yes. ~ SI
A70. These boys have the cold dope on the future. ~ SIERS
A72. This is a pair of trousers without the "s" on the end. It's pretty hard so we'll tell you that the first four letters spell what a dog does when he is hot. ~ PANTALOON
A74. Under a name that ma and the old man didn't give you. ~ INCOGNITO
A76. This one's a little bit high-hat, means magnificent. ~ PALATIAL
A80. The most important person in the world. ~ I
A81. The kind of drink you go to Montreal for (pl.). ~ ALES
A82. The repetition of a letter which is the way an Englishman pronounces the place where a lot of us are going when we die. ~ LL
A83. What Dad calls himself when he has a junior (abr.). ~ SR
A84. Young bears. ~ CUBS
A86. What some people have nerve enough to call a Ford. ~ CAR
A87. A little word that air castles are built on. ~ IF
A89. One of those poetic personal pronouns. ~ YE
A90. An abbreviation for a disease—that people go to Denver to cure. ~ TB
A91. You've met people who think this is what they are. ~ IT
A94. A Western State where some of the men still believe that Solomon showed good judgment. ~ UTAH
A96. It's hard to write this one because it means spoken. ~ ORAL
A98. To put on the feed-bag. ~ DINE
A99. A liquid food that some people inhale. ~ SOUP
A100. This last one's Ritzy too. It's what your words do from your mouth. ~ EMANATE

D1. The article of apparel that made people call young ladies flappers. ~ GALOSH
D2. Something we descend from, theoretically at least. ~ APE
D3. A kind of a voice not so good when described as "whisky." ~ TENOR
D4. The edition of a paper that tells all about a thing as soon as it happens. ~ EXTRA
D5. We all slip on one of these sooner or later. ~ BANANAPEEL
D6. What comes before beauty. ~ AGE
D7. Over some doors. ~ TRANSOM
D8. What any four drunks think they are capable of when they sing about "Sweet Adeline." ~ HARSONY
D9. Pronounced the same as buy. ~ BY
D10. Just one. ~ AN
D11. One of those pests who is always minding your business. ~ MEODLER
D12. Twice nothing. ~ OO
D13. When "a young man's fancy lightly turns to thought of love." ~ SPRING
D14. One who betrays. ~ EETRAYER
D18. A degree that students work (?) four years for—(not the third). ~ AB
D19. You and I. ~ WE
D23. Supposedly the richest man in the world (gives away nickels and everything). ~ ROCKEFELLER
D25. How most girls think they dance. ~ BEAUTRFULLY
D26. A pet name for the brain factory at New Haven. ~ ELI
D30. What ninety-nine out of a hundred bosses say when asked for a raise. ~ NO
D31. Too much of this is the bane of a stout woman's existence (abr.). ~ WT
D32. The only things besides promises that Congressmen hand out freely. ~ SEEDS
D33. No well behaved telephone should do this after you get to bed. ~ RING
D38. Stars (Latin). ~ ASTRA
D39. The thing that makes cats so essential to violins. ~ GUT
D42. Your one best pal (according to the Mammy songs). ~ MA
D43. What no woman is in an argument. ~ REASONABLE
D44. What any amount of candy given to children is about ten minutes later. ~ EATEN
D45. What the Giants probably did to their luck after the last game. ~ CURST
D47. When you tear your hair and gnash your teeth, you do this. ~ RANT
D49. A sailor. ~ GOB
D50. A lot of the mines that they sell stock in don't have much of this. ~ ORE
D52. A mythical maid changed by Juno into a heifer. ~ IO
D54. The way beer was drawn from kegs in the good old days. ~ SIPHONED
D59. You have to have two of these to be a good marathon runner. ~ LIG
D62. The number of times opportunity knocks. ~ ONCE
D64. What a girl says when she means yes. ~ NO
D65. The only sure way to get a thing done. ~ DO
D66. What you hate to see your mother-in-law do to her trunk when she honors (?) you with a visit. ~ UNPACKS
D68. Most of us have enough of these to insure us a through ticket to the bad place. ~ SINS
D69. Where you stay when you can't go out. ~ IN
D71. A New England State (abr.). ~ RI
D73. You may use any word starting with "A" here that you want to (we used ankles). ~ ANKLES
D75. Can be made into a stew without the use of alcohol. ~ OYSTER
D77. The Southern State where Opelika is located—if that means anything to you (abr.). ~ ALA
D78. Not in the dictionary—The initials of A. Sap. ~ AS
D79. You know me—. ~ AL
D85. The place in the basement where the black diamonds are kept. ~ BIN
D87. An abbreviation for the country that spaghetti made famous. ~ IT
D88. The fourth note in the scale that some moron named harmonic. ~ FA
D90. You do this with a shuttle—(nothing at all to do with Times Square). ~ TAT
D92. This one is a common exclamation. ~ OH
D93. One more letter like these and you have an empire that you can't see. ~ KK
D94. Great minds have been baffled by how high this one is. ~ UP
D95. A masculine pronoun. ~ HE
D96. Where your clothes should be when you are out in public. ~ ON
D97. Abbreviation for rear-admiral. Just why they call him rear is the question. ~ RA


Warnings:
Note: The black squares include cross-hatched ones that spell HA HA in two corners (clue 37 Across); the solid ones spell JUDGE. The puzzle has one-letter entries (20 Across = K, 80 Across = I) in the holes of the letters; the clue numbering follows the printed puzzle, not standard numbering rules. Solution not available: it would have run in the 29 November 1924 issue, whose archive.org scan is incomplete.
Grid isn't symmetric: 18 square pair(s) differ (r1c2/r15c25, r1c3/r15c24, r1c4/r15c23, r1c24/r15c3, r3c3/r13c24, r3c4/r13c23, r3c8/r13c19, r3c9/r13c18). Check those black squares against the scan, or confirm the design is asymmetric.

